<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles III Philip, Elector Palatine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1661">1661</a>–<a href="/years/1742">1742</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Charles Philip · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles III Philip was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a> from 1716 to 1742, the last ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuburg-an-der-donau">Neuburg</a> line. Before his accession he fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> as an imperial general and governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. After a quarrel with the Protestants over the Church of the Holy Spirit in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>, he moved his residence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a> in 1720 and began the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> palace there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-iii-philip-elector-palatine.jpg" alt="Charles III Philip, Elector Palatine" width="320" height="514" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Philipp von der Schlichten · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl_Philipp,_Kurf%C3%BCrst_(1716-1742).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Thirty Years' War Charles I Louis rebuilt the administration, and in 1720 <b>Charles III Philip</b> moved the residence to Mannheim, laid out on a grid around a vast new palace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>When Elector <b>Charles Philip</b>, the last of the Neuburg line, died in 1742, his lands passed to Charles Theodore of Palatinate-Sulzbach, who in 1777 also inherited Bavaria.</blockquote><a href="/state/palatinate-neuburg">Palatinate-Neuburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuburg-an-der-donau">Neuburg an der Donau</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.73,11.18&amp;year=1713" aria-label="Neuburg an der Donau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4809 462 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charles III Philip, Elector Palatine"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5259 732h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5259 732h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.73,11.18&amp;year=1713">What was here in 1713? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
